 @fabAB912  Exactly, UP TO...I cannot believe people still fall for all this marketing bs even after all these years. I mean, it's not even that they claimed something that is not true, it's just that they picked the most favorable scenario to get those numbers, which is what each company does of course.

I suspected it was going to be ray tracing performance uplift comparison, not traditional rasterization. Don't get me wrong, these numbers are impressive overall, but not as impressive as all the hype would let you to believe.

That's why it's always important to wait for independent benchmarks and draw conclusions from that, not from keynotes.

